Message-ID: <agUyyzjsJwc.9056PsVUbhUJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <agUyyzjsJwc.bXQLwiT5YlwJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <agUyyzjsJwc.bXQLwiT5YlwJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/agUyyzjsJwc/m/9056PsVUbhUJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Thanks for the help Bob, your input was extremely useful! Adding the conditional statement to avoid the INFs going to increment_log_prob fixed the massive slow down.

I'll post the updated model here for future reference.

mixture_model <- '

  data {
    int<lower=0> N; // sample size
    real x[N]; // observations

    real a; // parameter of the zero assumption
  }
  parameters {
    real<lower=0,upper=1> theta;
    real mu_1;
    //real mu_2;

    real<lower=0> v;
    real<lower=0> sigma;
  }
  model {

    real ps[2];
    mu_1 ~ normal(0, 1);
    //mu_2 ~ normal(0, 1);
    v ~ gamma(4, 2); // mean 2, variance 1
    sigma ~ gamma(1, 1); // mean 1, variance 1

    for(n in 1:N){
      ps[1] <- log(theta) + normal_log(x[n], mu_1, sigma);

      if(x[n] < -a || x[n] > a){

        ps[2] <- log1m(theta) + student_t_log(x[n], v, 0, 1) -
          student_t_cdf_log(-a, v, 0, 1) - log(2);

        // - log(2) to normalise accounting for both tails
        // this assumes that mu_2 is close to 0
        // it is exact when mu_2 = 0

        // ps[2] <- log1m(theta) + student_t_log(x[n], v, mu_2, 1) -
        //   student_t_cdf_log(-a, v, mu_2, 1) - log(2);

        increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(ps));

      } else {
        increment_log_prob(ps[1]);
      }
    }
  }
'

Fitting it to simulated data with:

set.seed(314)
n <- 25000
x.mix <- sample(1:2, n, replace = TRUE, prob = c(0.9, 0.1))
x.1 <- rnorm(n)
x.2 <- rt(n, 2)
a <- 2
n.A <- sum(x.2 < a & x.2 > -a)
while(n.A > 0){
  # print(n.A)
  x.2[x.2 <= a & x.2 >= -a] <- rt(n.A, 2)
  n.A <- sum(x.2 <= a & x.2 >= -a)
}
x <- rep(NA, n)
x[x.mix == 1] <- x.1[x.mix == 1]
x[x.mix == 2] <- x.2[x.mix == 2]

Results in:

Inference for Stan model: mixture_model.
2 chains, each with iter=1000; warmup=500; thin=1;
post-warmup draws per chain=500, total post-warmup draws=1000.

           mean se_mean   sd      2.5%       25%       50%       75%     97.5% n_eff Rhat

theta      0.90    0.00 0.00      0.90      0.90      0.90      0.90      0.91   386 1.00
mu_1       0.00    0.00 0.01     -0.02     -0.01      0.00      0.00      0.01   485 1.01
v          2.09    0.00 0.06      1.99      2.06      2.09      2.13      2.21   409 1.00
sigma      1.00    0.00 0.01      0.99      0.99      1.00      1.00      1.01   322 1.00
lp__  -44234.00    0.08 1.43 -44237.42 -44234.71 -44233.67 -44232.95 -44232.24   358 1.00

Samples were drawn using NUTS(diag_e) at Wed Aug  6 11:49:39 2014.

For each parameter, n_eff is a crude measure of effective sample size,
and Rhat is the potential scale reduction factor on split chains (at
convergence, Rhat=1).

And this is done in ~100 seconds for 1000 iterations (per chain).

I seem to consistently overestimate the degrees of freedom of the t distribution (hence underestimating the tail probabilities) but I don't think this is a major issue.

As for the comments in your reply:
-Yep, I fitted the full bayes model, not the empirical bayes.
-On the particular model choice: I first thought of fitting a single t distribution to the data and just use that, I fitted that and did not get great results. Then I looked at the p-values under the N(0,1) null:

 <https://lh6.googleusercontent.com/-ZUG6MMBaXjw/U-IMk7RgVcI/AAAAAAAAbV4/G5PE7sBzo4c/s1600/hist_aux.png>

so I then thought the two group model might actually be appropriate (sorry for not being able to share the data).

Thanks again for the help!

