You are right Herra! My bad :). I was looking for a log_sum_exp() somewhere. It's probably better to use that than explicitly code up all the exponents and sums, as log_sum_exp() is numerically more stable.

On Friday, 8 August 2014 10:28:07 UTC-7, Herra Huu wrote:

> Luc,
>
> Isn't that exactly what he is doing (well, implicitly)?
>
> He is expressing the mixing weights as a product of two probabilities given by parameters x_prob and z_prob. Thus, the final mixing weights in the model are: (1-x_prob[i])*(1-z_prob[i]), x_prob[i]*z_prob[i], (1-x_prob[i])*z_prob[i] and x_prob[i]*

> (1-z_prob[i]). In the code, the term log(x_prob[i]) is written as bernoulli_log(1, x_prob[i]) and log(1-x_prob[i]) as bernoulli_log(0,x_prob[i]). The original model could be equivalently written as (the main difference is the function log_sum_exp, which should be numerically more stable than taking the exp +sum directly)
>

> transformed parameters {
>   vector[4] lp_parts[nx, nz];
>   for (i in 1:nx) {
>     for (j in 1:nz) {
>       lp_parts[i, j, 1] <- (log1m(x_prob[i]) + log1m(z_prob[j]) + bernoulli_log(k[i, j], alpha));
>       lp_parts[i, j, 2] <- (log(x_prob[i]) + log(z_prob[j]) + bernoulli_log(k[i, j], alpha));
>       lp_parts[i, j, 3] <- (log1m(x_prob[i]) + log(z_prob[j]) + bernoulli_log(k[i, j], beta));
>       lp_parts[i, j, 4] <- (log(x_prob[i]) + log1m(z_prob[j]) + bernoulli_log(k[i, j], beta));
>     }
>   }
> }
> model {
>   for (i in 1:nx)
>     for (j in 1:nz)
>       increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(lp_parts[i,j]));
> }
>
>

