Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

This is a simplified example from a bivariate censoring problem which
does not admit such a simple formulation, hence the need to model it
this way (see [1]. For the univariate case I could, of course, use
Stan's truncated distros, but the original problem came up for a more
complicated model and I want to keep things similar.

In any case, z_aux has an improper/flat \propto 1 prior, which means
that it does not contribute to the posterior, so it is harmless. I have
a prior on z anyway.

Best,

Tamas

[1] https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/stan-users/PZE0tkwJ2A4

On Tue, Aug 19 2014, Luc Coffeng <lucco...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Sorry, I spoke/wrote too soon. Should have read your model code more
> carefully.
>

> The discrepancy may actually may due to the fact that your are assuming

> different distributions for z in the data-generating process and in the
> model. You use left-truncated log-normal for data generation, whereas you
> assume that z = m + exp(z_aux), where z_aux follows an improper uniform
> prior. I'm not sure those two things are the same. Why not model z directly
> as z ~ lognormal(0,1) [m,]; ?

