From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Tue Aug 19 15:47:16 2014
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Tue, 19 Aug 2014 15:47:16 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Stan throws error in population-averaged longitudinal GR IRT model.
Message-ID: <v_m0g2fv3Hg.8SRm1uloePwJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <v_m0g2fv3Hg.-I7z5wHL9WYJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <v_m0g2fv3Hg.1g0ZNRquTFwJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <v_m0g2fv3Hg.-I7z5wHL9WYJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/v_m0g2fv3Hg/m/8SRm1uloePwJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Ah, I see what's going on.  You always get the warning
with a local variable because there's no way to infer all that
can happen to it before it gets to sampling statement.

In your case

 parameters {
>    vector[T-1] mu_theta_raw;
> }
> model {
>    vector[T] mu_theta;
> ...
>
>    mu_theta[1] <- 0.0;
>    for (t in 1:(T-1))
>       mu_theta[t+1] <- mu_theta_raw[t];
> ...
> }

Do you really want to assign mu_theta[1] a prior?  It's set to 0.
You should be able to just vectorize and replace

> >   mu_theta[t] ~ normal(m_mu_theta, var_mu_theta);

with

  mu_theta_raw ~ normal(m_mu_theta, var_mu_theta);

You can still assign to mu_theta.  The parameters value are set before
entering the model block, so the above will be well defined.

- Bob

