Subject: Re: Stan for myriads of parameters in a linear regression model
Message-ID: <G421wRgpSXI.UewVyN2kGvcJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <G421wRgpSXI.4DL9hmiNY0sJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <G421wRgpSXI.4DL9hmiNY0sJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/G421wRgpSXI/m/UewVyN2kGvcJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Have you tried it? 50,000 parameters isn't a problem per se as long as you have enough RAM. We try to avoid hacks, but perhaps you can make things go faster by doing your own sparse multiplication, as in this model block below:

data {
  int<lower=1> N;
  int<lower=1> K;
  vector<lower=0,upper=2>[K] X[N];
  real y[N];
}
parameters {
  vector[K] beta;
  real<lower=0> sigma;
}
model {
  vector[N] mu;
  for(n in 1:N) {
    mu[n] <- 0;
    for(k in 1:K) if(X[n][k] != 0) mu[n] <- mu[n] + X[n][k] * beta[k];
  }
  y ~ normal(mu,sigma);
  // priors
}

Your model is probably not that simple, but that is the basic idea.

Ben

