Update on chain mixing:

With more informative priors the mixing and n_eff look quite good now. R_hats are ~=1.

  alpha ~ normal(1.0, 3.0);
   for (j in 1:J) {
         kappa[j] ~ normal(0.0, 2.5);
      }

On Thursday, August 21, 2014 2:17:38 PM UTC+2, Shamil Sadigov wrote:
> I have been wondering how to specify kappa[j] to be of varying length K[j]-1 in an array of size J (syntax-wise).
> In the likelihood statments I  have the index for kappas go until K[j]-1, but in the parameter declaration, I am not sure how to define an array of length J made of vectors of (varying) length K[j] - 1. Right now the code declares all kappa[j]s to be of size max(K[j] - 1), which is =6. Some priors for kappa then don't have the corresponding likelihood, I can see how this might cause an improper posterior.
>
> At different time points the max realized response is different, although the possible max is 7 for all items X1-X7.
>
>
>     T1 T2 T3 T4 T5 T6 T7
> X1  6  6  6  5  5  6  6
> X2  6  6  6  6  6  6  6
> X3  7  6  6  6  7  6  6
> X4  7  6  6  6  6  6  6
> X5  6  6  6  7  6  6  6
> X6  7  7  6  7  6  6  6
> X7  6  6  6  6  6  6  6
>
> - I don't want to model a separate intercept kappa_{ijt} for each time point - only for each level of each variable kappa_{ij}. Would that still be possible with varying max level?
>
> -Shamil
>
>
>
> On Wednesday, August 20, 2014 7:10:40 PM UTC+2, Bob Carpenter wrote:
