Hi, Avi, thanks for sending. I’m passing this on one to stan-users because I think others will be interested.  I looked at the post and their stan model looked kinda weird, with those uniform(0,1e5) and uniform(0,1e8) priors.  I can’t imagine that these are good ideas.  So I expect the model could be cleaned up.

I do think there’s a general problem, in Bugs or Jags or Stan, that if you put down a model that’s not well-defined, problems can occur.  In some sense this is a byproduct of the great flexibility of all these programs, that they allow users to put together a model like tinkertoys, and then anything can happen. From a statistical standpoint, I just can’t see uniform(0,1e8) ever making sense.  I think the model should be on the log scale.

But I’m not sure what the general solution is here. Jennifer and I already recommend in our book that people do the log transformation for positive parameters, but there are always going to be users who are just learning statistics, or who are following published analyses that might have statistical flaws.

The idea that someone is running 250,000 iterations on such a simple model, that’s upsetting to me.  Yet another motivation for us to write that Statistics Using Stan book!

Andrew

